(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-cam-nutrition-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 1 — Master Exam (Form C)","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which body-composition measurement method uses hydrostatic (underwater) weighing?","choices":["Skinfold measurement","Bioelectrical impedance","Dual X-ray photon absorptiometry (DXA)","Densitometry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Densitometry uses underwater (hydrostatic) weighing, which is very accurate — it relies on the fact that fat is less dense than lean tissue, using body weight and body volume to calculate body density. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six classes of nutrients presented in lecture?","choices":["Fiber","Lipids","Minerals","Water"],"answer":0,"exp":"The six classes of nutrients are carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, vitamins, minerals, and water. Fiber is a type of complex carbohydrate, not a separate nutrient class of its own. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 9 (6 Classes of Nutrients)"},{"q":"What carbohydrate-related dietary approach helps manage blood sugar in diabetes, per lecture?","choices":["Favor refined carbohydrates over complex carbohydrates, since they provide quicker bursts of usable energy.","Carbohydrate type doesn't matter, since only total daily calorie count affects blood sugar control.","Favor complex carbohydrates over refined carbohydrates, since they digest more slowly and avoid spikes.","Eliminate all carbohydrates from the diet to avoid any blood sugar spikes entirely."],"answer":2,"exp":"Favoring complex carbohydrates over refined carbohydrates is recommended, since complex carbs are slower to digest and help avoid blood sugar spikes. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which dietary change does the lecture actually recommend for improving the current North American eating pattern?","choices":["Choose more nutrient-dense foods","Choose animal proteins over plant proteins","Choose solid fats over plant oils","Decrease the quantity and variety of fruits and vegetables"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture recommends choosing more nutrient-dense foods as one of several improvements needed in the current eating pattern. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 33 (Assessing the Current North American Diet)"},{"q":"What BMI range defines \"severe thinness\" within the underweight category, per lecture?","choices":["17.00-18.49","<16.00","16.00-16.99","18.50-24.99"],"answer":1,"exp":"Severe thinness is defined as a BMI below 16.00, the lowest of the three underweight subcategories. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"When should a nutrition screen be completed on a hospitalized patient, per lecture?","choices":["Only at discharge","Within the first week of admission","Within 24 hours of admission","Only if the patient reports unintentional weight loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"The nutrition screen should be completed within 24 hours of admission, typically as part of nursing admission. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the recommended protein intake during pregnancy according to the lecture?","choices":["About 175 grams per day, representing roughly 50% of total daily calories.","Routine protein supplementation on top of a normal diet.","Between 20 and 35% of total daily calories should come from protein.","1.1 g/kg/day, or about 71 g/day, roughly 20% of daily total calories."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives 1.1 g/kg/day (about 71 g/day), roughly 20% of total daily calories, as the pregnancy protein recommendation. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the \"DETERMINE\" acronym, the first letter \"D\" stands for which warning sign?","choices":["Dehydration","Dental loss","Depression","Disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the first D in DETERMINE stands for disease. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does the MyPlate icon in the lecture divide the plate among food groups?","choices":["Fruits and vegetables cover half; grains about one-fourth; protein the remaining one-fourth; with a cup of dairy alongside","Protein covers half the plate, with grains filling the rest","The plate is divided equally into four food groups with no dairy","Grains cover half the plate, with vegetables as a side"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: MyPlate shows fruits and vegetables as half the plate, grains and protein each about one-fourth, plus a cup of dairy. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 57"},{"q":"What folic acid dose does ACOG recommend during an ongoing pregnancy for a woman without a history of a prior neural tube defect-affected pregnancy?","choices":["4 mg/day","0.6 mg/day","0.4 mg/day","450 mg/day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states ACOG recommends 0.6 mg of folic acid during pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What do the five \"Blue Zones\" identified by Dan Buettner and National Geographic have in common?","choices":["The highest numbers of centenarians with low rates of heart disease, diabetes, obesity, and cancer","The highest total populations in the world","The highest average daily calorie intakes","The lowest average life expectancies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Blue Zones are regions with the most centenarians and low rates of those chronic diseases. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What BMI criteria make a patient a general candidate for weight-loss medication, per lecture?","choices":["BMI ≥18.5 alone, with no comorbid conditions required at any level","BMI ≥30, or BMI ≥27 with at least one obesity-associated comorbid condition","BMI ≥25 alone, regardless of whether any comorbid conditions are present","BMI ≥40 alone, with no lower threshold option available for comorbid patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Candidates for weight-loss medication generally have a BMI ≥30, or a BMI ≥27 with at least one obesity-associated comorbid condition. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A pregnant patient has severe dietary restrictions. Which fetal risk is specifically associated with maternal dietary restriction, as distinguished from the risks of maternal obesity in the lecture?","choices":["Macrosomia","Childhood obesity","Low fetal birth weight","Fetal neural tube defects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states maternal dietary restrictions increase risk of poor fetal development and low fetal birth weight. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the Recommended Dietary Allowance for carbohydrate in adults, per the lecture?","choices":["130 grams per day","30 grams per day","500 grams per day","No Recommended Dietary Allowance is set for carbohydrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the Recommended Dietary Allowance for carbohydrate is 130 grams per day for adults. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 35"},{"q":"What is the single most significant contributor to basal metabolic rate, per lecture?","choices":["Chronological age alone","Total body weight","Dietary fat intake","Lean body mass (LBM)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lean body mass (LBM) is described as the most significant contributor to basal metabolic rate, and it varies greatly between individuals of the same body weight. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which of the following can cause a potassium deficiency, per the slide?","choices":["Laxative abuse","Excess dietary potassium intake","High blood pressure","Vitamin D toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The slide lists laxative abuse, along with chronic diarrhea, vomiting, alcohol abuse, eating disorders, and very low-calorie diets, as causes of potassium deficiency, with symptoms including muscle cramps, confusion, constipation, and irregular heartbeat. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 151 (Potassium: Deficiency)"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in lecture as a recommended improvement to the North American diet?","choices":["Increase intake of solid fats over plant oils.","Increase sodium and added sugar intake.","Decrease the variety and quantity of fruits and vegetables consumed.","Choose plant sources of protein rather than animal proteins."],"answer":3,"exp":"Lecture lists choosing plant protein sources over animal proteins, along with choosing plant oils over solid fats, moderating sugar/salt/alcohol, and increasing fruit and vegetable variety, as needed improvements to the North American diet. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 33 (Assessing the Current North American Diet)"},{"q":"What weight gain pattern does the lecture describe for a typical pregnancy?","choices":["About 1 pound per week throughout the whole of pregnancy, including the entire first trimester.","About 5 to 10 pounds total gained in the first trimester, then minimal gain afterward.","1-5 pounds total in the first trimester, then approximately 1 pound per week in the second and third trimesters.","No specific first-trimester target; weight gain begins only in the second trimester."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This matches the lecture's stated weight gain pattern. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"According to the lecture's patient education on caloric requirements, how many additional kcal/day are recommended during the second trimester?","choices":["No additional calories","+450 kcal/day","+340 kcal/day","+680 kcal/day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states the second trimester recommendation is an additional 340 kcal/day. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Of hospitalized adults who will experience a nutrition decline during admission, what proportion does lecture cite?","choices":["1% to 5%","10% to 65%","100%","70% to 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"10% to 65% of hospitalized adults will experience a nutrition decline during their admission, per the cited prevalence data. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which benefit of omega-3 fatty acid intake during pregnancy is supported in the lecture?","choices":["Consistently demonstrated, well-established long-term neurocognitive benefits for the developing child.","A meaningfully reduced risk of fetal goiter developing during gestation.","Support of normal fetal brain and eye development, and association with a lower risk of preterm birth.","Direct prevention of neural tube defects during early fetal development."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states omega-3 fatty acids support normal fetal brain and eye development and are associated with a lower risk of preterm birth. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"According to the lecture's diagnostic approach, what should a physical examination look for in suspected food allergy?","choices":["Elevated liver enzymes found on standard laboratory blood testing","Signs of an allergic reaction such as rash, itching, or intestinal bloating","Abnormal heart rhythm on cardiac auscultation","Weight loss over the preceding several months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states the physical examination should 'look for signs of an allergic reaction (rash, itching, intestinal bloating, etc.).' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed among malnutrition's associated implications in hospitalized patients?","choices":["Decreased overall risk of developing infection","Improved potential for physical rehabilitation","A shorter overall hospital length of stay","Falls, pressure ulcers, and delayed wound healing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Malnutrition is associated with increased falls, pressure ulcers, delayed wound healing, infections, and death, plus decreased rehabilitation potential. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How many additional calories per day and how much daily protein does the lecture recommend for a breastfeeding mother?","choices":["340 kcal/day additional and 71 g/day protein","450 kcal/day additional and 27 g/day protein","500 kcal/day additional and 70 g/day protein","600 kcal/day additional and 85 g/day protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states breastfeeding requires 500 additional kcal/day and 70 grams/day of protein. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as an indication that a person's weight is not healthy, per lecture?","choices":["Lower-body (gynoid) fat distribution only","A waist circumference below the cutoff for either sex","Normal blood glucose levels","Upper-body (android, \"apple-shaped\") fat distribution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Upper-body (android/apple-shaped) fat distribution is listed as an indication that weight is not healthy, along with hypertension, elevated non-HDL cholesterol, family history of obesity, and elevated blood glucose. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The lecture explains that letting an infant continue with a bottle of carbohydrate-rich fluid promotes dental caries because bacteria do what?","choices":["Block fluoride from reaching the teeth","Increase saliva production","Secrete acids that dissolve tooth enamel","Directly consume the tooth's calcium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: bacteria secrete acids that dissolve enamel when teeth are bathed in carbohydrate-rich fluid. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 268"},{"q":"The lecture describes a \"thrifty phenotype.\" Which set of traits best matches it?","choices":["Requires more calories and resists storing any body fat","Maintains a constant weight regardless of intake","Requires fewer calories for metabolism and stores body fat easily","Burns energy quickly when overeating with little drop during fasting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the thrifty phenotype needs fewer calories and stores fat easily, historically aiding survival through famine. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What hormones are described as encouraging lower-body (gynoid, \"pear-shaped\") fat distribution, per lecture?","choices":["Testosterone and growth hormone","Thyroid hormone and glucagon","Insulin and cortisol","Estrogen and progesterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Estrogen and progesterone are described as encouraging gynoid (lower-body, \"pear-shaped\") fat distribution. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What two nutrition-screen questions does lecture specifically highlight for identifying at-risk hospitalized adults?","choices":["Current medication list and any known drug allergies","Resting blood pressure reading and current heart rate","Patient's height measurement and current shoe size","Have you lost weight without trying, and has your appetite/eating decreased?"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nutrition screen asks about unintentional weight loss (how much, over what time frame) and decreased appetite/eating. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"The lecture lists general readiness signs for solid foods. Which matches?","choices":["Reaching about 25 pounds and refusing all milk","Sleeping through the night for a week","Having a full set of molars","Reaching about 13 pounds and consuming more than 32 ounces of formula daily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: readiness signs include reaching about 13 pounds and taking more than 32 ounces of formula daily or breastfeeding 8 to 10 times in 24 hours. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 216"},{"q":"What acceptable body fat percentage range is given for women, per lecture?","choices":["23% to 31%","13% to 21%","Over 35%","Under 10%"],"answer":0,"exp":"An acceptable body fat percentage for women is 23% to 31%. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"According to the lecture's definitions, what distinguishes a food allergy from a food intolerance?","choices":["A food allergy involves an immune response, while a food intolerance does not.","A food allergy is caused by bacteria, while a food intolerance is caused by viruses.","A food allergy occurs only in children, while a food intolerance occurs only in adults.","A food allergy causes weight loss, while a food intolerance causes weight gain."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines food allergy as 'an adverse reaction to food that involves an immune response' and food intolerance as 'an adverse reaction to food that does not involve an allergic reaction.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"The lecture cautions that dietary supplements and alternative-medicine products for older adults carry which concerns?","choices":["Clear and consistent superiority over obtaining the same nutrients from whole food","Questionable purity and quantity, possible health risks, and contraindications with current medications","Guaranteed purity with full insurance coverage","No possible interaction with current medications"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture warns of questionable purity and quantity, possible health risks, and drug contraindications, preferring nutrients from a healthy diet. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In the lecture's Grilled Chicken Sandwich example, the 45 grams of protein contributed how many of the sandwich's 490 total kcal?","choices":["180 kcal","225 kcal","405 kcal","130 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Protein provides ~4 kcal/gram, so 45 g × 4 = 180 kcal, matching the lecture's worked example exactly. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 23 (Calculating Calories)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, up to what share of the total calories in breast milk comes from fat?","choices":["Nearly 0%","Up to about 55%","About 10%","About 35% to 40%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: breast milk provides up to about 55% of total calories as fat, which is calorie-dense. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 180"},{"q":"The lecture identifies which group as the largest at risk for malnutrition?","choices":["Infants under 6 months","People aged 65 and older, particularly those in long-term care facilities and hospitals","Adolescents going through their pubertal growth spurt, especially athletes","Adults aged 30 to 40 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: adults 65 and older, especially in long-term care and hospitals, are the largest group at risk for malnutrition. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A fasting blood glucose level greater than 125 mg per 100 mL of blood indicates which condition?","choices":["Metabolic syndrome","Ketosis","Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hyperglycemia is defined as blood glucose greater than 125 mg per 100 mL (fasting) — high blood glucose from a lack of glucose control. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 32 (Regulating Blood Glucose)"},{"q":"Per ASPEN significant-weight-loss guidelines cited in lecture, which pairing of timeframe and percent loss is correct?","choices":["1 week: 2%","30 days: 2%","6 months: 5%","1 week: 10%"],"answer":0,"exp":"ASPEN's significant-weight-loss guideline pairs 1 week with 2% loss (also: 30 days with 5%, 3 months with 7.5%, and 6 months with 10%). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which group of foods must be clearly identified on labels under the 2006 Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act, per the lecture?","choices":["Only the grains wheat, rye, and barley","Sulfites, MSG, and artificial coloring agents","Milk, eggs, fish, shellfish, peanuts, tree nuts, wheat, and soy","Red wine, tomatoes, and ripe pineapples"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these eight foods as the major allergens the 2006 Act mandates be clearly identified on labels. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"The lecture notes a decline in hydrochloric acid, intrinsic factor, and digestive enzymes with age most directly lowers absorption of which vitamin?","choices":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Vitamin K","Vitamin D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: reduced hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor lower absorption of vitamin B12. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What percentage of total daily energy needs does the thermic effect of food (TEF) account for, per lecture?","choices":["15% to 30%","40% to 50%","8% to 15%","60% to 80%"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thermic effect of food accounts for approximately 8% to 15% of total daily energy needs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What three electrolyte abnormalities characterize refeeding syndrome?","choices":["Hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia","Hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia, hypermagnesemia","Hyponatremia, hypochloremia, hypocalcemia","Hypernatremia, hyperkalemia, hypercalcemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Refeeding syndrome is characterized by hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Per the Food and Nutrition Board's list of exceptions to standard protein needs, dialysis patients require approximately how much protein per kilogram of body weight, compared to the standard 0.8 g/kg RDA?","choices":["About 0.6 g/kg of body weight","About 2.5 g/kg of body weight","About 1.7 g/kg of body weight","1.1 to 1.4 g/kg body weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Dialysis patients need about 1.1–1.4 g/kg body weight, above the standard 0.8 g/kg RDA, per the slide's list of exceptions to standard protein needs (which also includes endurance athletes and the elderly). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 86 (RDA for Protein)"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish \"life span\" from \"life expectancy\"?","choices":["Life span applies to women and life expectancy applies to men","Life span is the maximum years a human can live; life expectancy is the average years a person born in a given year and place is expected to live","Life span is the average years lived; life expectancy is the biological maximum","Both terms mean the same number of years for any individual"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: life span is the biological maximum, while life expectancy is the statistical average for a birth cohort. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"For childhood constipation, the lecture notes it may result from an intolerance to which food and recommends more fiber and fluids?","choices":["Fresh fruit","Cow's milk","Iron-fortified cereal","Breast milk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: childhood constipation may result from intolerance to cow's milk, and more fiber, fluids, and activity are recommended. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 391"},{"q":"What does the lecture state a blood test determines in food allergy diagnosis?","choices":["The presence of antibodies in blood that bind to food antigens tested","The patient's total white blood cell count on a complete blood count","Blood glucose response after ingesting the suspected food","Liver function markers measured following allergen exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines the blood test as determining 'the presence of antibodies in blood that bind to food antigens tested.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which constipation treatment does the lecture specify requires supervision by a primary care provider?","choices":["Increasing fluid intake","Laxatives","Regular exercise","Establishing a regular bowel routine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture specifies that laxatives, with primary care provider supervision, can lessen constipation. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which limits does the lecture's healthy eating pattern set for saturated fat, added sugars, and sodium?","choices":["Sodium under 500 milligrams and no fat limit","Saturated fat under 10% of calories, added sugars under 10% of calories, and sodium under 2,300 milligrams","Saturated fat under 50% of calories and no sodium limit","No limit on added sugars as long as calories are met"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the pattern limits saturated fat and added sugars to less than 10% of calories each and sodium to less than 2,300 milligrams. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 56"},{"q":"The lecture states cow's milk is not suitable for infants for which reason?","choices":["Its protein is too high and its mineral content is not suitable","It contains no fat at all","It is too low in every nutrient","It contains added fluoride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cow's milk is not tolerated because its protein is too high and its mineral content is unsuitable for infants. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 191"},{"q":"The lecture states an infant's length increases by roughly what amount by the end of the first year?","choices":["About 50%","About 10%","About 100%","About 200%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: length increases about 50% by the end of the first year. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The lecture cautions against overfeeding infants because excess intake can do what?","choices":["Permanently raise the child's metabolic rate","Improve organ development","Reduce future appetite","Increase the number of adipose (fat) cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: overfeeding increases the number of adipose cells, which can persist as obesity develops. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which treatment for constipation does the lecture specifically note should be used 'with supervision of primary care provider'?","choices":["Increased fiber consumption","Regular exercise","Establishing a regular bowel routine","Laxatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states 'Laxatives, with supervision of primary care provider can lessen constipation.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"According to the lecture, approximately how many childhood emergency room visits per year are caused by food allergies?","choices":["9,200","920,000","42,000","92,000"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states food allergies cause '92,000 childhood ER visits and 84 child fatalities per year.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why does the lecture specifically caution about iodine status during pregnancy, beyond simply stating its RDA?","choices":["Most prenatal vitamins do not contain much, if any, iodine","Iodine is toxic in any amount during pregnancy","Iodine RDA decreases during pregnancy compared to non-pregnant adults","Iodine is only obtainable through supplementation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture specifically notes that most prenatal vitamins do not contain much, if any, iodine, despite its importance. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"On a nutrition facts food label, the 'Calories' figure (capital C) is numerically equivalent to which unit discussed in the lecture?","choices":["kilocalorie (kcal)","calorie (lowercase c)","kilogram","joule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture states that energy on food labels is expressed as Calories (capital C), which is the same as kcal — 1,000 calories = 1 kcal = 1 (food) Calorie. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 22 (Calories Defined)"},{"q":"Which vegetarian diet pattern includes plant foods and dairy products, but excludes eggs, fish, and meat?","choices":["Lactovegetarian","Pescatarian","Vegan","Lacto-ovo-vegetarian"],"answer":0,"exp":"A lactovegetarian eats plant foods and dairy products, but no eggs, fish, or meat. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 92 (Food Planning for Vegetarians)"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as a naturally occurring food constituent that can cause food intolerance?","choices":["Constituents naturally found in foods like red wine, tomatoes, and pineapples","Antibodies in the blood that bind to specific food antigens","Flattened villi lining the wall of the small intestine","IgE-mediated immune activation triggered by an allergen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists red wine, tomatoes, and pineapples as constituents of certain foods that can cause food intolerance. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the primary difference between hard water and soft water, according to the lecture?","choices":["Hard water is unsafe to drink; soft water is always safe","Hard water contains high calcium and magnesium; soft water contains high sodium","Hard water comes only from wells; soft water comes only from municipal supplies","Hard water contains high sodium; soft water contains high calcium and magnesium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hard water contains high levels of calcium and magnesium (soap doesn't lather well in it; 89% of homes have hard water), while soft water contains a high level of sodium (and produces cleaner-feeling clothes). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 140 (What Is the Difference Between Hard and Soft Water?)"},{"q":"Which statement about hard versus soft water is accurate, per the slide?","choices":["Soft water is unsafe to drink, while hard water is always considered safe","Hard water contains high levels of calcium and magnesium; soft water contains a high level of sodium","Hard and soft water differ only in their fluoride content levels","Hard water contains high levels of sodium; soft water contains high levels of calcium and magnesium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The slide defines hard water as containing high levels of calcium and magnesium (present in about 89% of U.S. homes, and it makes soap lather poorly), while soft water contains a high level of sodium and produces cleaner-rinsing laundry. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 140 (What Is the Difference Between Hard and Soft Water?)"},{"q":"What is parenteral nutrition?","choices":["Any diet supervised by a registered dietitian.","Nutrition fed into the GI tract via a tube.","Nutrition delivered via the vein (IV).","Oral nutrition supplements taken between meals."],"answer":2,"exp":"Parenteral nutrition is IV nutrition, delivered directly into a vein. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
